! Floating-point diagnostic jb_af1c, routine F210 "STORE ROUNDED TEST
! ROUTINE", section C "ACCUMULATOR FLAGS TO MEMORY FLAGS", sub-tests
! C1-C2 (docs/jb_af1c_floating-point.pdf pp.111-112, octal
! 016111.40-016130.40, where F210D "TESTS OF INDICATORS" begins).
!
! The oracle for the other half of p.96's storage-word sentence: "The
! sign of the accumulator, as changed by the sign modifiers, is placed
! in storage bit 60. The accumulator flag bits are placed in storage
! bits 61-63." F210A checked bit 60 with its BZB questions; nothing so
! far has looked at 61-63 at all.
!
!   C1  CM1111 sets ROS to all ones FIRST, then Z,$L / Z,$R / Z,$SB
!       zero the accumulator and its sign byte. After SRD(U) ROS, the
!       memory flags at ROS+.61 must be ZERO -- so the stored flags
!       have to come from the accumulator and cannot be whatever was
!       already in the word. Then it asks that the accumulator's own
!       flags at 10.5 are still zero.
!   C2  the mirror: all ones everywhere, flags 7, and both questions
!       expect 7.
!
! C1 is the sharper of the two. Pre-loading ROS with ones is what
! makes it a test of copying rather than a test of leaving alone -- a
! model that simply never wrote bits 61-63 would pass C2 and fail C1.
!
! FSR3 is the listing's own label at its real address, 016111.40 --
! the routine's exit branches back to it.
!
! ROS (022355) is the routine's scratch word, not a pool constant.
! Framework substitutes as everywhere else in this series: SERS (octal
! 1304) -> FAIL halt, SEN (octal 1310) -> scratch. Constant from the
! pool p.176. PASS sits at 016130.40, where F210D begins.
!
! Instructions: CM1111, Z, SRD(U), CT0011, CT1100, C1111, SIC, B,
! BRGZ.
! verify_fp_arith_f210c_listing.py checks every word bit-for-bit.
!
! Observed: both passed on the first run, and C1's question is live --
! forcing the UNFLAGGED branch to write flags 3'b111 instead of the
! accumulator's halts C1 with SIC storing 016116.40, right after its
! first BRGZ, while F210A3-A4 stays green.
!
! Worth recording precisely which branch that is. The same forcing
! applied to the FLAGGED branch changes nothing anywhere: C1's
! accumulator is zeroed by Z,$L / Z,$R / Z,$SB so EF is 0 and it takes
! the unflagged path, and C2's accumulator is all ones so it takes the
! flagged path but already expects flags of 7. So C2 cannot see the
! flagged branch's flag handling at all, and C1 cannot see it either.
! Nothing in F210 does. That is a real gap, and it is narrow: it is
! the one case where SRD returns a survivor's flags rather than the
! accumulator's, and no sub-test distinguishes them because the two
! agree everywhere F210 looks.

! -- SSIP framework substitutes --
        SLC, (8)1304.0
SERS    BEW, $
        SLC, (8)1310.0
SEN     DD(BU,64,8), 0

! -- the routine's scratch word --
        SLC, (8)22355.0
ROS     DD(BU,64,8), 0

! -- constants (pool p.176) --
        SLC, (8)022006.0
FPK1    DD(BU,64,8), (8)1777777777777777777777

! -- code (verbatim, at the listing's addresses) --
        SLC, (8)16111.40
FSR3    CM1111(BU), ROS                        ! F210C1: accumulator flags + 0
        Z, $L
        Z, $R
        Z, $SB
        SRD(U), ROS
        CT0011(BU,3), ROS+.61                  ! check memory flags + 0
        SIC, SEN
        BRGZ, SERS
        CT0011(BU,3), 10.5                     ! do acc. flags remain + 0
        SIC, SEN
        BRGZ, SERS
        B, $+.32                               ! F210C2: accumulator flags + 7
        C1111(BU,64), FPK1, 64
        C1111(BU,64), FPK1
        CM1111(BU,8), 10.0
        SRD(U), ROS
        CT1100(BU,3), ROS+.61                  ! check memory flags + 7
        SIC, SEN
        BRGZ, SERS
        CT1100(BU,3), 10.5                     ! do acc. flags remain + 7
        SIC, SEN
        BRGZ, SERS
PASS    BEW, $                                 ! 016130.40: where F210D begins
        END, FSR3
